# metrics.json — 20261011-2235-second-20261011-2132-spacex-91a0-e4cc (corrected record)

Every number below is computed from the raw rows by `work/metrics.py` (`python3 work/metrics.py <rows.json>`),
regenerated by `git apply work/patch.diff` (the first run's hlive mode), `cargo build --release -p xtask`, then
`$CARGO_TARGET_DIR/release/xtask universe --hlive --seed S --rtf 100 --seconds T --members M`
(T=20→2000 game-s, T=10→1000; the m1600 run additionally applies `work/clamp-1600.diff`, which raises the
pre-existing `--members` clamp 400→4000 in the xtask harness). Machine: i7-4790, 8 vCPU, 15 GB, shared.

Corrections against the first run's metrics.json (its checker's findings, verified here):
- "kicks.fired_in_2000_game_s: 0" was FALSE: the rows show 1113–9196 kicks per 2000 game-s run, firing in
  bursts to the last game-second. The true invariant: bits per ship stay flat DESPITE the kicks.
- "catalogue.ships_peak: 275" was FALSE: peak ships are 360–366 (m400), 65 (m48), 1415 (m1600).
- The catalogue mechanism is per ship CREATED, not per fleet member shed: seed 7 keeps all 400 members in the
  frame to game-second 2000 and still gains 390 catalogue entries.

Reproduction level (this machine, load shared with sibling runs): headline quantities agree across
published-vs-regenerated runs to ≤ 0.4 % (kicks 7443 vs 7443 vs 7443; 9160 vs 9196; 5277 vs 5287; 1113 = 1113;
catalogue endpoints equal except s99 396 vs 397; bits and slopes identical to 2 decimals). Row-level integers
are budget-sensitive: the engine advances under a 5 ms/frame wall budget, so the tick count at a matched
game-second drifts by a few ticks between runs (first divergence s1-m400: game-s 75, tick 7340136 vs 7340138),
and ship counts shift within ±3 afterwards. Row-exact identity is therefore NOT part of the claim; every
headline number below is quoted as a range over reruns (2 samples per config + 1 determinism repeat for s1).

